WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Generated code should be reviewed as carefully as code written by a teammate.

2
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:10.423
The speed of generation does not reduce the need for engineering judgment.

3
00:00:10.423 --> 00:00:18.344
In fact, speed can make review more important, because a large amount of plausible code can arrive before the

4
00:00:18.344 --> 00:00:22.096
developer has built a mental model of what changed.

5
00:00:22.096 --> 00:00:25.014
Begin with the scope of the patch.

6
00:00:25.014 --> 00:00:26.265
Which files changed?

7
00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:27.932
Were unexpected files added?

8
00:00:27.932 --> 00:00:31.268
Did dependencies, generated artifacts, or configuration files appear?

9
00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:35.854
Did the assistant remove working behavior while solving the visible task?

10
00:00:35.854 --> 00:00:41.273
Scope review answers a basic question: is this the change you asked for,

11
00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:45.025
or did the patch expand beyond the approved boundary?

12
00:00:45.025 --> 00:00:50.028
This first pass often catches the highest-risk surprises before line-by-line review begins.

13
00:00:50.028 --> 00:00:51.696
Next, review the design.

14
00:00:51.696 --> 00:00:57.532
The code should belong in the correct layer, reuse existing abstractions, preserve established interfaces,

15
00:00:57.532 --> 00:01:01.285
and remain no more complicated than the task requires.

16
00:01:01.285 --> 00:01:06.287
A small feature should not introduce a framework, a global state system,

17
00:01:06.287 --> 00:01:10.873
or a new service boundary unless there is a clear reason.

18
00:01:10.873 --> 00:01:15.042
Architecture mistakes can be harder to fix than syntax mistakes.

19
00:01:15.042 --> 00:01:17.127
Then inspect the implementation details.

20
00:01:17.127 --> 00:01:22.963
Check input validation, error handling, boundary conditions, resource cleanup, names, comments, and duplicated logic.

21
00:01:22.963 --> 00:01:29.217
Ask whether bad input, empty data, timeouts, permission failures, or partial writes have been considered.

22
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:34.637
If the assistant explains a change, compare the explanation with the actual code.

23
00:01:34.637 --> 00:01:42.558
A confident explanation can still describe what the code was intended to do rather than what it really does.

24
00:01:42.558 --> 00:01:45.893
Reading generated code also protects your own understanding.

25
00:01:45.893 --> 00:01:53.397
A project can appear to advance quickly while the human gradually loses the ability to explain its behavior.

26
00:01:53.397 --> 00:01:58.400
Review is the moment when generated output becomes an intentional engineering decision.

27
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.986
If you cannot explain a change, simplify it, ask for clarification,

28
00:02:02.986 --> 00:02:07.155
or reject it before it becomes part of the codebase.
